NOTICE — what is in this repository

The MIT grant above is the licence. Everything below is description, not a
condition on it.

Every product in this collection is fictional. Nocturne, Tandem, Quill, Bodega,
Nimbus, Meridian, Quiver, Trellis, Loopfeed, Quorum, Vista Studio, Halo, Verity,
Bastion, Onyx Card, Trailmark, Momentum, Wellspring, Codenest, Dwell, Airwave,
Bet Slip, Appfront, Vend, Pings and the rest are invented companies with invented
names, invented logos and invented colour palettes. Every home-screen icon is
drawn from primitives in tools/icon-specs.js. No screenshot, logo, wordmark or
asset belonging to a real company is reproduced, redistributed or shipped here,
and tools/rebrand.js --check fails the build if one appears.

The deliberate exception is assets: token tickers and their artwork (BTC, ETH,
SOL, USDC) are real, because a ticker names what an asset is rather than who
makes it, and a wallet that cannot name what it holds is broken rather than
careful. The line is company versus asset. Company names are invented; asset
tickers are not. Stock tickers on invented positions ARE renamed, because a
fabricated gain attached to a named public company is a claim about that company.

Where a real name is unavoidable outside the mocks — the README naming GitHub,
the install sheet naming Safari or iOS — that is nominative use: naming a real
thing in order to tell you a true fact about it.

No affiliation with, endorsement by, or connection to any real company is claimed
or implied. Any trademarks that appear in documentation belong to their owners.

---

ACCEPTABLE USE

Not a licence condition, and not enforceable as one. It is what this project is
for, stated plainly, because the tool would otherwise be easy to misread.

These are interface studies. They exist so you can look at how a good banking,
payments, trading or analytics screen is put together, take one apart, and change
it. Editable values and a phone view are there so the study is a study rather
than a picture.

They are not evidence of anything. Do not use them to represent to another person
that you hold money, an asset, a position, a payout, a transaction or an account
that you do not hold. Presenting a mock-up as a real balance, a real payment, a
real return or a real payout in order to obtain money, credit, investment,
goodwill or anything else of value is fraud in most places, and the fact that the
image came from here is not a defence.

Every screen carries a "LARP · not a real app" mark by default, and the switch
that removes it says in plain words what removing it means. Turning it off is
allowed and sometimes reasonable. What you do with an unmarked screenshot is
yours, and so is the consequence.
